{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "The dominant service aspect (topic) driving each airline review",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "dominant_topic",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Directly proposes the review's central aspect (staff, food, seat comfort, etc.), naming the focus concept the analyst should investigate."
    },
    {
      "name": "review_sentiment",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Polarity is a coherent facet that pairs with dominant_topic, enabling analysis of which aspect drives positive vs negative reactions."
    },
    {
      "name": "review_length_category",
      "supports_focus": false,
      "rationale": "A generic structural/metadata bucket of text length; it does not centre on any review-content aspect and reads as boilerplate noise rather than a facet of the implied focus."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.6,
  "structure_quality": 0.5,
  "focus_actionability": 0.72,
  "rationale": "Two of the three columns cohere around a plausible, data-inferable focus — the dominant service aspect of each review — with sentiment providing a useful cross-cutting facet that turns topic identification into something actionable (which aspects worth analyzing correlate with satisfaction). This is a defensible proposed focus given the vague query. However, review_length_category is a mechanical text-length bucket that dilutes coherence and adds no facet of the content focus, so the set is not tightly centred. The structure is thin: only topic + sentiment genuinely build around the focus, giving a serviceable but underdeveloped predictor/facet set rather than a rich one. The focus itself is non-trivial and investigable, hence moderate actionability, but the grab-bag element and limited depth keep scores middling."
}
